= Levantar una columna

A column is not recruited so much as *grown*: a base of support thick enough to feed and hide a standing armed unit, a commander the villages already trust, weapons enough to arm the willing. You have [+ n_columnas +] of the five columns the movement can sustain at once — more than that and the command structure frays, the supply lines cross, the Colonel gets too many trails to follow. Where does the new one rise?

*(A column only takes root where the water is deep. Raising one where apoyo is thin gives the army a gift.)*

- @lev_guerrero: Guerrero — the Costa Grande.
- @lev_oaxaca: Oaxaca — the Isthmus.
- @lev_valle: the Valle — an urban comando.
- @lev_chihuahua: Chihuahua — the northern sierra.
- @lev_jalisco: Jalisco — the west.
- @root: Not yet. A column raised before its base is a funeral with a flag.
- @easy_discard

@lev_guerrero
choose-if: parque >= 2
unavailable-subtitle: Arming a new column takes materiel — at least 2 parque.
on-arrival: {! var s=0; for(var i=1;i<=5;i++){ if(!Q['col'+i+'_zona']){s=i;break;} } if(s){ Q['col'+s+'_zona']="guerrero"; Q['col'+s+'_nombre']="Col. de la Costa Grande"; Q.pres_guerrero=(Q.pres_guerrero||0)+8; Q.columnas_formadas=1; } Q.parque-=2; Q.moral_columnas+=2; Q.expediente+=2; !}

A new column takes the field on the Costa Grande, where the mountains and the memory of Lucio do half the recruiting. It will grow or it will draw the army's eye — usually both. The dot is on the map now, and the map, from here, is the war.

- @root: Continuar.

@lev_oaxaca
